Answer: Yes, multiple objects can be created from the same class. Example:

```java
Dog dog1 = new Dog();
Dog dog2 = new Dog();
```

Here, two separate Dog objects are created. Each object has its own memory for instance variables such as name, breed, and cost.

Answer: When two objects are created, separate memory is allocated for each object. Example:

```java
Dog dog1 = new Dog();
Dog dog2 = new Dog();
```

The first object may be created at one memory location, and the second object may be created at another memory location. Both objects contain their own instance variables and can hold different values.

Answer: The reference of an object is collected when the object needs to be accessed multiple times. Example:

```java
Dog dog1 = new Dog();
dog1.name = "Rocky";
dog1.breed = "Bulldog";
dog1.cost = 9999;
dog1.eat();
dog1.sleep();
dog1.bark();
```

The object reference dog1 is used multiple times to access the properties and methods of the same object.

Answer: The two ways are: Create the object, collect its reference, and access it multiple times. Create the object and access it directly without collecting its reference. Example of creating an object with a reference:

```java
Dog dog1 = new Dog();
dog1.eat();
dog1.sleep();
dog1.bark();
```

Example of creating an object without a reference:

```java
new Dog().bark();
```

Answer: Every time new Dog() is written, a new Dog object is created. Example:

```java
new Dog().bark();
new Dog().bark();
```

Two different objects are created. The second statement does not access the object created by the first statement.

